# The kernel Thalyx needs, and nothing else.
#
# Applied on top of `allnoconfig`, which starts from nothing. That direction
# matters: a distribution kernel with things switched off still contains
# everything nobody remembered to switch off, and nobody can say what is in it.
# Here, what is on is what somebody put here.
#
# Every line here is checked against the .config that comes out the other side,
# and the build stops if one of them did not survive. That check is not
# ceremony: nine of these options were asked for and silently dropped, because
# `olddefconfig` discards anything whose dependencies are unmet and says nothing.
# Among them were BPF_LSM and DEBUG_INFO_BTF — the kernel would have booted
# perfectly and thalyx-lsm could never have attached to it.
#
# So an option that is here only because another option needs it says so, and
# names what breaks without it. Deleting one of those lines is not a cleanup.

# --- being able to boot at all ---
CONFIG_64BIT=y
CONFIG_SMP=y
CONFIG_PRINTK=y
CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF=y
CONFIG_MULTIUSER=y
CONFIG_POSIX_TIMERS=y
CONFIG_FUTEX=y
CONFIG_EPOLL=y
CONFIG_SIGNALFD=y
CONFIG_TIMERFD=y
CONFIG_EVENTFD=y
CONFIG_SHMEM=y
CONFIG_AIO=y

# --- the console we are watched through ---
#
# Two of them, and a real PC has only the second.
#
# ttyS0 is a serial port, which is what QEMU gives and what no laptop made this
# century has. Until 2026-08-07 it was the only console this kernel knew, and the
# consequence was the third item on the risk list in `Construccion-del-ISO.md`: a
# PC would boot the ISO correctly and **show nothing at all** — a failure that
# reads as "it does not work" and is "you cannot look".
#
# tty0 is the screen, through the framebuffer the firmware already set up. See the
# EFI framebuffer section below for why that particular one.
CONFIG_TTY=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250=y
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_PRINTK_TIME=y
CONFIG_VT=y
CONFIG_VT_CONSOLE=y

# --- the screen, so a machine with no serial port can be watched ---
#
# `FB_EFI` is the framebuffer the firmware has **already configured** before the
# kernel starts: UEFI leaves a linear framebuffer behind through its Graphics
# Output Protocol, and this driver adopts it. That is why there is no graphics
# driver here for any actual card. Thalyx needs to draw text, not to bring up a
# GPU, and a real driver for every chip on the market is a userland's worth of
# code — which is what this image exists not to have.
#
# The cost is stated rather than hidden: the resolution is whatever the firmware
# chose, and it cannot change. That is the correct trade for a machine whose
# entire interface is lines of text.
#
# The three below FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE are derived symbols asked for anyway, so
# `config-check` reports it if any of them fails to materialise. FONT_8x16 is the
# one that decides whether anything is legible: a console with no font compiled in
# initialises, reports nothing wrong, and draws a blank screen.
CONFIG_FB=y
CONFIG_FB_EFI=y
CONFIG_FRAMEBUFFER_CONSOLE=y
CONFIG_FONT_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_FONT_8x16=y
CONFIG_SYSFB=y

# --- the keyboard, which on a real PC is USB and not emulated ---
#
# QEMU's keyboard arrives through the serial console, so none of this has ever
# been needed and none of it can be tested by a VM. A PC has two possible
# keyboards and both are here, because which one a given machine presents is not
# something this build can know:
#
#   xHCI    every USB port on anything made since about 2010
#   EHCI    USB 2 controllers on older machines, and some firmware's fallback
#   i8042   the PS/2 controller, which many laptops still expose their built-in
#           keyboard through even when there is no PS/2 socket anywhere
#
# INPUT is selected by VT and asked for anyway: it is the layer the virtual
# terminal reads keys from, and a kernel with a USB keyboard driver and no input
# core has a keyboard that works and types into nothing.
CONFIG_INPUT=y
CONFIG_INPUT_KEYBOARD=y
# HID_SUPPORT is a `menuconfig` and not a driver: `drivers/hid/Kconfig` opens with
# it and everything else in that file, plus `usbhid/Kconfig`, lives inside its
# `if`. It is `default y`, which is why nobody writing a config by hand ever meets
# it — and `allnoconfig` starts from nothing, so a default of y is a default of n.
#
# Without this line HID, HID_GENERIC and USB_HID are not rejected: they are
# invisible, so `olddefconfig` drops all three and `config-check` names three
# options when one line is missing. That happened on 2026-08-07, on the first build
# after the keyboard was added, and the three names it printed all pointed away from
# the cause.
CONFIG_HID_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_HID=y
CONFIG_HID_GENERIC=y
CONFIG_USB_HID=y
CONFIG_USB_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_USB=y
CONFIG_USB_PCI=y
CONFIG_USB_XHCI_HCD=y
CONFIG_USB_XHCI_PCI=y
CONFIG_USB_EHCI_HCD=y
CONFIG_USB_EHCI_PCI=y
CONFIG_SERIO=y
CONFIG_SERIO_I8042=y
CONFIG_KEYBOARD_ATKBD=y

# --- the disks a real PC has, which are not virtio ---
#
# `thalyx install` writes a partition table and two filesystems onto a block
# device, and until now the only block device this kernel could see was QEMU's.
# An installer that cannot see the disk it is installing onto is not an installer.
#
# NVMe needs MSI: it is not a nicety, the driver allocates its queues around
# message-signalled interrupts and a device without them either performs terribly
# or does not attach. PCI_MSI is off under `allnoconfig` and nothing else here
# would have asked for it.
#
# SATA goes through the SCSI layer, which is why BLK_DEV_SD is here: without it
# an AHCI controller is found, its ports are probed, and no `/dev/sda` appears.
CONFIG_PCI_MSI=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_NVME=y
CONFIG_SCSI=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_SD=y
CONFIG_ATA=y
CONFIG_SATA_AHCI=y

# --- booting with nothing in front of the kernel ---
#
# `Criterio-de-Salida-Fase-1.md`: Phase 1 ends with an ISO that turns a PC with
# no operating system into a Thalyx machine. Until now QEMU was the bootloader —
# `-kernel` and `-initrd` are the host loading this into memory because nothing
# else would.
#
# The obvious answer is GRUB, and the obvious answer is forbidden:
# `Filosofia-Fundacional.md` allows one program, and a bootloader is a second
# one. It is the same shape as the `bpftool` hole — an outside tool in the image
# because something was needed.
#
# So there is no bootloader. A kernel built with EFI_STUB *is* a UEFI
# application: the firmware loads the bzImage directly as \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI,
# with nothing in between. EFI on x86 depends on ACPI, and EFI_STUB on
# RELOCATABLE; both are here because config-check reports what is asked for and
# cannot see what nobody asked for.
CONFIG_ACPI=y
CONFIG_EFI=y
CONFIG_RELOCATABLE=y
CONFIG_EFI_STUB=y

# The command line, built in, because there is no bootloader to pass one.
#
# Firmware launching \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI hands the stub no load options at
# all, so everything the machine needs to know at boot has to be compiled in.
#
# **Without CMDLINE_OVERRIDE, on purpose.** It is the option a person reaches
# for here — it makes the line unchangeable by whatever launched the kernel —
# and it would have broken the one thing that currently passes. `make run` boots
# through QEMU with `-append ... thalyx.store=/dev/vda`, and OVERRIDE discards
# the bootloader's line entirely. Stage 16 of verify.sh would have come up with
# no store, every store check would have failed, and the cause would have been a
# kernel option three files away from the failure.
#
# Without it the two are concatenated: **this line first, then the bootloader's**,
# which `arch/x86/kernel/setup.c` does with the comment "append boot loader
# cmdline to builtin". The same kernel boots both ways, which is the property that
# matters — the machine being tested has to be the machine that ships.
#
# ## Two consoles, and the order is the whole point
#
# The kernel prints to **every** `console=` it is given. What the *last* one
# decides is `/dev/console`, which is the one file Thalyx's session talks through
# — so the order below is not cosmetic, it is which screen a human sees.
#
#   booted by firmware   nothing is appended, so `tty0` is last:
#                        the session is on the PC's screen, which is what a PC has
#   booted by QEMU       `-append console=ttyS0` lands after this line, so the
#                        serial wins and stage 16 does not notice any of this
#
# That is why `tty0` goes second and not first, and it is the reason the order in
# `arch/x86/kernel/setup.c` was checked in the source rather than assumed. Reversed,
# a PC with no serial port would depend on the 8250 driver failing to register
# `ttyS0` so that `tty0` kept `/dev/console` — true on most machines, undocumented,
# and not a thing to hang the only visible output of the system on. Decided by
# Cesar on 2026-08-07.
#
# `thalyx.store=` is **not** here, and that absence is on purpose. It names a
# device, this line is compiled once, and the disk is `vda` under QEMU and
# `nvme0n1` or `sda` on a real PC. A machine booted from the ISO alone will
# report that no store was named, which is what `store_disk.rs` already says and
# is the honest answer. Solving it is the store's own step, and the answer is
# probably the filesystem label `mkfs.btrfs -L thalyx-store` already writes:
# asking for a name is not the same as mounting the first disk that answers,
# which is what that module refuses to do. See `Construccion-del-ISO.md`.
CONFIG_CMDLINE_BOOL=y
CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=ttyS0 console=tty0 lsm=capability,bpf panic=-1"

# --- the root filesystem is a cpio archive, built into the kernel ---
#
# It used to be a second file handed over by QEMU. On a boot medium that would
# be a second file to find, and the decree counts files: the medium carries one,
# and it is the kernel with Thalyx inside it. `make -C image count` extended to
# the thing you plug in.
#
# The path is not here because it is absolute and belongs to whoever is
# building. The Makefile appends CONFIG_INITRAMFS_SOURCE at configure time and
# checks it separately, for the reason this whole file exists: asking is not
# having.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y
CONFIG_RD_GZIP=y
CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y
CONFIG_TMPFS=y
CONFIG_TMPFS_POSIX_ACL=y
CONFIG_TMPFS_XATTR=y
CONFIG_PROC_FS=y
CONFIG_SYSFS=y

# --- Btrfs is mandatory: Journal-y-Snapshots.md, and restore depends on it ---
CONFIG_BTRFS_FS=y
CONFIG_BTRFS_FS_POSIX_ACL=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_CRC32C=y
CONFIG_LIBCRC32C=y

# --- the disk the store lives on ---
#
# VIRTIO_MENU and BLK_DEV have no function of their own: they are the menus the
# two drivers live inside, and without them QEMU hands the machine a disk that
# no driver claims. VIRTIO itself has no prompt at all — VIRTIO_PCI selects it,
# and asking for it directly does nothing.
CONFIG_PCI=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_MENU=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK=y
CONFIG_BLOCK=y

# --- a module's confinement is a cgroup ---
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_PIDS=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_SCHED=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF=y

# --- the sandbox: namespaces, seccomp, idmapped mounts ---
CONFIG_NAMESPACES=y
CONFIG_UTS_NS=y
CONFIG_IPC_NS=y
CONFIG_PID_NS=y
CONFIG_NET_NS=y
CONFIG_USER_NS=y
CONFIG_SECCOMP=y
CONFIG_SECCOMP_FILTER=y
# IPC_NS is unreachable without System V IPC compiled in, so a module would have
# shared the host's IPC objects while every other namespace looked correct.
CONFIG_SYSVIPC=y

# --- thalyx-lsm. Without these there is no enforcement and Thalyx says so ---
#
# The four below BPF_JIT are what BPF_LSM needs and does not announce: it
# depends on BPF_EVENTS, which is a hidden symbol that only appears once
# kprobes exist inside the tracing menu. Ask for BPF_LSM without them and it
# does not come back rejected — it comes back absent.
CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITYFS=y
# The socket hooks are a separate option, and its absence is what stopped the
# first boot that got this far — 2026-08-04. The machine said it plainly:
#
#   no  thalyx-lsm  this kernel does not expose `bpf_lsm_socket_connect`
#
# `bpf_lsm_<hook>` symbols are generated from `include/linux/lsm_hook_defs.h`,
# and every socket hook in that file sits inside `#ifdef
# CONFIG_SECURITY_NETWORK`. Without it the symbol is not absent by accident:
# it was never compiled, so there is nothing for the program to attach to.
#
# CONFIG_SECURITY was here and CONFIG_SECURITY_NETWORK was not, and nothing
# noticed, because `config-check` compares what was *asked for* against what
# came out. It cannot see something nobody asked for. That is what
# `hook-check` in the Makefile is for now: it asks the object which kernel
# symbols it attaches to and looks for them in the kernel just built.
CONFIG_SECURITY_NETWORK=y
CONFIG_BPF=y
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=y
CONFIG_BPF_JIT=y
CONFIG_PERF_EVENTS=y
CONFIG_FTRACE=y
CONFIG_KPROBES=y
CONFIG_KPROBE_EVENTS=y
CONFIG_BPF_LSM=y
# And the machinery an LSM program is *attached* with, which is a separate
# thing from the hook existing. Missing on 2026-08-04, one boot after
# SECURITY_NETWORK, and the symptom was a single errno:
#
#   no  thalyx-lsm  attaching `thalyx_socket_connect`: Resource busy
#
# BPF attaches to an LSM hook with a trampoline. `register_fentry` asks
# `ftrace_location()` whether the function is ftrace-managed; without dynamic
# ftrace it is not, so the kernel falls back to patching the text itself — and
# `__bpf_arch_text_poke` expects to find the five-byte NOP that FUNCTION_TRACER
# puts at the top of every function. Its own words, from the Kconfig help:
# "a compiler feature to insert a small, 5-byte No-Operation instruction at the
# beginning of every kernel function".
#
# Without that NOP the memcmp against the expected bytes fails, and the return
# is `-EBUSY` — which reads as something else holding the hook, and nothing
# was. `CONFIG_FTRACE=y` above is only the menu; it emits no NOP.
#
# The three below FUNCTION_TRACER are derived symbols that cannot be set
# directly. They are asked for anyway so `config-check` reports it if any of
# them fails to materialise: DIRECT_CALLS is the one that actually decides
# whether a trampoline can be installed, and it is three dependencies away
# from the option a person would think to turn on.
CONFIG_FUNCTION_TRACER=y
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_FTRACE=y
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_FTRACE_WITH_ARGS=y
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_FTRACE_WITH_DIRECT_CALLS=y
# BTF is how thalyx-lsm's ten hooks find this kernel's structures, and it is
# gated twice: DEBUG_INFO_BTF lives under DEBUG_INFO, which no option can turn
# on directly — a DWARF choice selects it, and that choice is only offered when
# DEBUG_KERNEL is on. Under allnoconfig none of that holds, so BTF was dropped
# and the only symptom would have been a watcher that never loads.
#
# It also needs pahole (Fedora: `dwarves`) at configuration time, v1.21 or
# newer. If pahole is missing the kernel does not complain; the option simply
# is not there, and the check in the Makefile is what turns that into an error.
CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF_TOOLCHAIN_DEFAULT=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_BTF=y
CONFIG_LSM="capability,bpf"

# --- networking, only so a module can be denied it ---
CONFIG_NET=y
CONFIG_INET=y
CONFIG_UNIX=y
